WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.193 --> 00:00:01.390
(upbeat jazz music)

00:00:01.390 --> 00:00:02.913
- [Chris] It is an art to make this dough

00:00:02.913 --> 00:00:04.020
because when you spend

00:00:04.020 --> 00:00:07.083
many hours making something
that people enjoy,

00:00:07.100 --> 00:00:08.730
it becomes a masterpiece.

00:00:08.730 --> 00:00:10.982
That is what makes it all worth while.

00:00:10.982 --> 00:00:12.210
- [Michael] Fresh pasta is more

00:00:12.210 --> 00:00:13.160
of a special occasion.

00:00:13.160 --> 00:00:14.530
You have to have practice at it.

00:00:14.530 --> 00:00:15.763
It's a labor of love.

00:00:15.800 --> 00:00:17.500
It's the smell of the
egg when you cook it,

00:00:17.500 --> 00:00:19.750
you can taste the egg and the flour,

00:00:19.750 --> 00:00:20.850
it's pretty darn good.

00:00:25.834 --> 00:00:28.834
(upbeat jazz music)

00:00:33.820 --> 00:00:36.024
- [Chris] I think of what
my father used to say,

00:00:36.024 --> 00:00:37.540
"if it's an egg noodle,

00:00:37.540 --> 00:00:39.047
that means you put egg in it."

00:00:39.570 --> 00:00:40.540
When you're making,

00:00:40.540 --> 00:00:42.303
like a fresh egg noodle dough,

00:00:42.660 --> 00:00:44.210
with a machine where you now

00:00:44.210 --> 00:00:45.583
have to put it together,

00:00:45.770 --> 00:00:48.287
it involves using your hands,

00:00:48.287 --> 00:00:50.497
your mind, and your heart.

00:00:50.497 --> 00:00:52.580
(laughs)

00:00:53.230 --> 00:00:55.460
We like to maintain quality

00:00:55.860 --> 00:00:57.993
with our raviolis and egg noodles.

00:00:58.390 --> 00:01:00.060
And the only way you can do that

00:01:00.060 --> 00:01:02.763
is by using quality ingredients.

00:01:03.680 --> 00:01:06.413
You really need to understand the dough,

00:01:06.830 --> 00:01:07.896
understand the weather.

00:01:07.896 --> 00:01:09.100
Is it too wet?

00:01:09.100 --> 00:01:10.053
Is it too dry?

00:01:10.340 --> 00:01:11.483
Do I add water?

00:01:11.500 --> 00:01:13.103
Do I add a little more flour?

00:01:13.480 --> 00:01:15.880
Basically have to be
consistent with that dough.

00:01:17.550 --> 00:01:19.123
That machine came from Italy,

00:01:19.150 --> 00:01:21.493
it's been in the store since 1950.

00:01:21.520 --> 00:01:22.735
I like to say sometimes,

00:01:22.735 --> 00:01:25.083
I'm part of that machine.

00:01:25.160 --> 00:01:28.250
In order to make that be
of the quality we want

00:01:28.250 --> 00:01:29.150
when we sell this.

00:01:31.620 --> 00:01:33.950
The most bought pasta
that we make and sell

00:01:33.990 --> 00:01:35.270
is taken back to the home.

00:01:35.270 --> 00:01:39.360
And I think the significance
of something like that

00:01:39.360 --> 00:01:43.323
is where unlike a store
bought package pasta,

00:01:43.740 --> 00:01:45.403
the texture, the flavor,

00:01:45.460 --> 00:01:46.683
is gonna be different.

00:01:46.700 --> 00:01:50.390
It has that homemade quality taste

00:01:50.470 --> 00:01:51.850
as if they were there,

00:01:51.850 --> 00:01:53.903
maybe making it with their grandmother.

00:01:56.210 --> 00:01:58.210
My grandmother had some recipes

00:01:58.210 --> 00:01:59.743
making pasta at home.

00:01:59.960 --> 00:02:01.311
And in 1935,

00:02:01.311 --> 00:02:04.423
they opened up Borgatti's
Ravioli &amp; Egg Noodles.

00:02:04.580 --> 00:02:07.683
This is actually the recipe, of course,

00:02:07.940 --> 00:02:09.430
my grandparents were using

00:02:09.430 --> 00:02:10.930
when they opened up the store.

00:02:15.571 --> 00:02:18.738
(bright violin music)

00:02:19.810 --> 00:02:21.600
It is an art to make this dough

00:02:21.600 --> 00:02:24.813
because you're involved with creating it,

00:02:24.870 --> 00:02:25.960
kind of designing it,

00:02:25.960 --> 00:02:26.973
finishing it,

00:02:27.040 --> 00:02:28.950
using your hands and mind

00:02:28.950 --> 00:02:29.912
and your heart.

00:02:29.912 --> 00:02:32.740
Pasta should feel very soft,

00:02:32.740 --> 00:02:34.010
but yet strong.

00:02:34.010 --> 00:02:35.773
It becomes a masterpiece.

00:02:42.630 --> 00:02:43.933
Been here 82 years.

00:02:44.130 --> 00:02:46.420
I came into the picture around 1976

00:02:46.420 --> 00:02:47.306
as an 18 year old.

00:02:47.306 --> 00:02:51.733
I think of it as a dream
of my grandparents.

00:02:51.733 --> 00:02:53.430
I'm just, you know,

00:02:53.430 --> 00:02:54.693
fulfilling their dream.

00:02:55.150 --> 00:02:56.523
It is a passion, a love,

00:02:56.610 --> 00:02:58.670
is when you spend many hours

00:02:58.670 --> 00:03:00.400
standing there making something

00:03:00.400 --> 00:03:01.823
that people enjoy.

00:03:01.830 --> 00:03:03.282
A customer would come back and say

00:03:03.282 --> 00:03:05.173
"I've had your noodles,

00:03:05.270 --> 00:03:08.580
I can't imagine ever buying
package pasta ever again."

00:03:08.580 --> 00:03:10.153
When you hear things like that,

00:03:10.240 --> 00:03:12.833
that is what makes it all worth while.

00:03:15.026 --> 00:03:18.443
(bright orchestra music)

00:03:28.288 --> 00:03:30.705
(jazz music)

00:03:35.347 --> 00:03:36.780
I always think of like,

00:03:36.780 --> 00:03:38.100
when it comes to food

00:03:38.319 --> 00:03:39.819
or when you think about pasta,

00:03:40.250 --> 00:03:42.513
it's something that brings us together.

00:03:42.910 --> 00:03:46.043
We share good times and
good moments, you know?

00:03:46.800 --> 00:03:48.390
I used to come here when I was young,

00:03:48.390 --> 00:03:50.650
help out, putting raviolis together

00:03:50.650 --> 00:03:51.996
and packing raviolis.

00:03:51.996 --> 00:03:54.228
When they first opened up the store,

00:03:54.228 --> 00:03:55.780
in order to shape the raviolis,

00:03:55.780 --> 00:03:57.040
they didn't have a machine.

00:03:57.040 --> 00:03:58.910
They were actually making these raviolis

00:03:58.910 --> 00:04:00.440
near the window of the store

00:04:00.440 --> 00:04:02.570
so people could see what was going on

00:04:02.570 --> 00:04:03.533
in this store.

00:04:03.930 --> 00:04:05.630
When it was selling in the 1935,

00:04:05.630 --> 00:04:07.220
when they opened up a
hundred little pieces,

00:04:07.220 --> 00:04:10.023
only cost you about one dollar.

00:04:12.180 --> 00:04:13.647
My father used to say,

00:04:13.647 --> 00:04:16.300
"use good quality ingredients.

00:04:16.300 --> 00:04:17.610
Focus on the past,

00:04:17.610 --> 00:04:18.793
what got you here?

00:04:19.160 --> 00:04:21.200
What made this place what it is?

00:04:21.200 --> 00:04:23.107
So always be mindful of that."

00:04:25.410 --> 00:04:27.260
What started out as a family business,

00:04:27.260 --> 00:04:29.183
continues as a family.

00:04:29.210 --> 00:04:30.650
Keeping it simple

00:04:30.660 --> 00:04:32.193
because at the end of the day,

00:04:32.210 --> 00:04:34.853
am I doing it to make the money?

00:04:35.190 --> 00:04:37.940
Or am I doing it because I know

00:04:38.020 --> 00:04:39.823
when people sit down to this,

00:04:40.070 --> 00:04:41.930
they'll say these are the best raviolis

00:04:41.930 --> 00:04:44.448
I ever had in my whole life?

00:04:44.448 --> 00:04:46.531
(laughs)

00:04:46.915 --> 00:04:49.915
(upbeat jazz music)

00:04:50.480 --> 00:04:51.313
With pasta,

00:04:51.313 --> 00:04:53.139
it's something that brings us together.

00:04:53.139 --> 00:04:56.100
I mean there's a variety of different ways

00:04:56.100 --> 00:04:57.740
noodles could be served these days;

00:04:57.740 --> 00:04:59.193
it's not always the same way.

00:04:59.280 --> 00:05:01.213
You just feel good and go oh boy.

00:05:01.751 --> 00:05:02.584
(laughs)

00:05:02.584 --> 00:05:05.501
(smooth jazz music

00:05:14.110 --> 00:05:15.070
- [Michael] Pasta is everywhere

00:05:15.070 --> 00:05:16.510
and Italian food is everywhere.

00:05:16.510 --> 00:05:18.400
But good pasta is something hard to find

00:05:18.400 --> 00:05:21.693
because there's so many
nuisances in pasta.

00:05:22.000 --> 00:05:23.800
You have to really focus on

00:05:23.800 --> 00:05:26.363
the equal parts of the flour and egg yolk.

00:05:28.340 --> 00:05:30.540
I grew up in Wisconsin
in a Norwegian family

00:05:30.810 --> 00:05:33.000
and so food was very
very important, always.

00:05:33.000 --> 00:05:34.520
Whether it was breakfast or dinner,

00:05:34.520 --> 00:05:36.320
there was something always
going on in the kitchens.

00:05:36.320 --> 00:05:37.230
When I started cooking,

00:05:37.230 --> 00:05:38.550
there was no Food Network,

00:05:38.550 --> 00:05:40.140
or it wasn't on TV like it is today.

00:05:40.140 --> 00:05:42.150
I remember watching Yan Can Cook

00:05:42.150 --> 00:05:44.120
and great chefs on the Discovery Channel

00:05:44.120 --> 00:05:45.020
as a young person.

00:05:45.150 --> 00:05:46.690
I was in college and made a decision

00:05:46.690 --> 00:05:48.360
that after a football injury

00:05:48.360 --> 00:05:49.780
that I was gonna become a chef.

00:05:49.780 --> 00:05:51.497
And when I told my father that he said,

00:05:51.497 --> 00:05:52.330
"that's fantastic,

00:05:52.330 --> 00:05:54.310
but how do you expect to make a living?"

00:05:54.890 --> 00:05:55.770
I started in the kitchen

00:05:55.770 --> 00:05:57.640
just to kinda see if I really liked it.

00:05:57.640 --> 00:05:59.640
And I remember going there the first day

00:05:59.690 --> 00:06:00.890
and being mesmerized

00:06:00.890 --> 00:06:03.370
by eggs and flour comin' together

00:06:03.370 --> 00:06:05.113
and be something such as pasta.

00:06:06.970 --> 00:06:08.380
Needless to say after the first day,

00:06:08.380 --> 00:06:09.493
I was ready to roll.

00:06:10.260 --> 00:06:11.093
Probably one of the things

00:06:11.093 --> 00:06:11.970
that attracted me the most

00:06:11.970 --> 00:06:13.460
was coming in early in the morning

00:06:13.460 --> 00:06:14.580
and working with pasta.

00:06:14.580 --> 00:06:17.631
It's something that I can
get my hands messy with.

00:06:17.631 --> 00:06:20.150
Never forget that cooking is a vocation,

00:06:20.150 --> 00:06:21.028
it's something that use

00:06:21.028 --> 00:06:22.423
and do with your hands.

00:06:26.520 --> 00:06:27.779
Obviously I love fresh pasta.

00:06:27.779 --> 00:06:29.020
I don't want people to think

00:06:29.020 --> 00:06:30.532
there's anything wrong
with dry pasta either.

00:06:30.532 --> 00:06:32.250
But fresh pasta in some instances

00:06:32.250 --> 00:06:33.610
is more of a special occasion.

00:06:33.610 --> 00:06:35.003
It's a labor of love.

00:06:35.030 --> 00:06:36.640
It doesn't happen instantly.

00:06:36.640 --> 00:06:38.020
You have to have practice at it.

00:06:38.020 --> 00:06:39.140
It's like doing anything,

00:06:39.140 --> 00:06:40.620
you have to enough repetition

00:06:40.620 --> 00:06:42.070
to know that when you touch it,

00:06:42.070 --> 00:06:42.903
it feels right.

00:06:42.903 --> 00:06:43.736
It feels dry.

00:06:43.736 --> 00:06:44.569
It feels humid.

00:06:44.569 --> 00:06:45.402
I can look at something

00:06:45.402 --> 00:06:47.140
and give it a spray of water

00:06:47.140 --> 00:06:48.490
because I know it needs it.

00:06:48.620 --> 00:06:49.660
Those types of things

00:06:49.660 --> 00:06:50.793
all come with time.

00:06:57.250 --> 00:06:58.470
I'm still perfecting my craft.

00:06:58.470 --> 00:06:59.340
You know we're constantly

00:06:59.340 --> 00:07:00.830
pushing boundaries of pasta,

00:07:00.830 --> 00:07:02.573
and we're having fun.

00:07:07.452 --> 00:07:09.869
(jazz music)

00:07:10.600 --> 00:07:11.467
The garganelli for example,

00:07:11.467 --> 00:07:12.370
is a dish

00:07:12.370 --> 00:07:13.830
that there's an old story.

00:07:13.830 --> 00:07:16.080
About how garganelli became garganelli

00:07:16.080 --> 00:07:17.650
is they were all laid out over

00:07:17.950 --> 00:07:19.710
the work surface

00:07:20.480 --> 00:07:22.100
and there was tortellini pieces of meat

00:07:22.100 --> 00:07:23.690
put on every one of the squares

00:07:23.930 --> 00:07:24.763
and a little cat

00:07:24.763 --> 00:07:26.623
ate all the filling off the squares.

00:07:27.090 --> 00:07:28.880
And so the chef is sitting there

00:07:28.880 --> 00:07:30.460
with all these squares of pasta

00:07:30.480 --> 00:07:31.313
and she didn't know what to do.

00:07:31.313 --> 00:07:32.360
So she grabbed a comb

00:07:32.360 --> 00:07:34.380
and she rolled them over a comb,

00:07:34.380 --> 00:07:36.080
so that's how that pasta happened.

00:07:37.980 --> 00:07:39.530
Now I don't know if there's any

00:07:39.870 --> 00:07:41.150
real truth to that story or not,

00:07:41.150 --> 00:07:42.253
but it sounds great.

00:07:43.700 --> 00:07:44.630
We toss it very simply

00:07:44.630 --> 00:07:47.333
with prosciutto and cream and parmigiano.

00:07:47.340 --> 00:07:49.210
The beauty of that pasta is the fact that

00:07:49.210 --> 00:07:50.750
it absorbs in the sauce quicker.

00:07:50.750 --> 00:07:52.890
You can smell the egg when you cook it

00:07:52.890 --> 00:07:53.723
and toss with cream,

00:07:53.723 --> 00:07:55.960
you can taste the egg and the flour.

00:07:55.960 --> 00:07:57.623
So it's all in the details.

00:07:59.040 --> 00:08:00.910
To sit down and eat it a
complete meal with pasta

00:08:00.910 --> 00:08:02.760
is something I don't do all the time,

00:08:03.270 --> 00:08:04.570
but it's pretty darn good.

00:08:06.037 --> 00:08:08.454
(jazz music)

00:08:16.617 --> 00:08:18.010
(sultry music)

00:08:18.010 --> 00:08:18.947
Pasta is something that

00:08:18.947 --> 00:08:21.620
is the cornerstone of the
Altamarea Group business.

00:08:21.620 --> 00:08:23.340
It's a cornerstone of what I do.

00:08:23.340 --> 00:08:25.940
It's what people come
to the restaurants for.

00:08:25.940 --> 00:08:28.480
The pasta has fulfilled so
many of my dreams already.

00:08:28.480 --> 00:08:29.750
Knowing that people are coming

00:08:29.750 --> 00:08:30.653
to eat your food,

00:08:30.653 --> 00:08:32.233
that's pretty cool.

00:08:32.310 --> 00:08:34.380
These people like came in on a train

00:08:34.380 --> 00:08:36.010
like an hour and a half from work,

00:08:36.010 --> 00:08:37.633
they go an hour and a half back.

00:08:37.720 --> 00:08:39.370
Like you better pinch yourself, right?

00:08:39.370 --> 00:08:40.690
It's really really something

00:08:40.690 --> 00:08:41.776
and those types of things

00:08:41.776 --> 00:08:44.689
are what keep me driving to make pasta

00:08:44.689 --> 00:08:47.363
as close to perfection as we possibly can.

00:08:48.880 --> 00:08:50.040
We're really sure

00:08:50.040 --> 00:08:51.113
that we're cooking for the people

00:08:51.113 --> 00:08:52.550
that we have in front of us.

00:08:52.550 --> 00:08:54.270
And the person behind the stove

00:08:54.270 --> 00:08:56.763
cooking the pasta has to
be passionate about it.

00:08:57.280 --> 00:08:58.617
And this is a pasta that is

00:08:58.617 --> 00:09:01.170
bone marrow, braised octopus,

00:09:01.170 --> 00:09:03.283
red wine, tomato, and basil.

00:09:03.320 --> 00:09:04.153
Proud of that dish

00:09:04.153 --> 00:09:05.173
and at the same time,

00:09:05.430 --> 00:09:07.126
you will never be able to take off, so.

00:09:07.126 --> 00:09:10.083
You're only as good as
your last pasta dish.

00:09:10.430 --> 00:09:11.570
That's what we like to say.

00:09:11.570 --> 00:09:12.870
I don't know anything else either though,

00:09:12.870 --> 00:09:13.703
do you know what I mean?

00:09:13.703 --> 00:09:15.097
It's just kind of what I do.

00:09:35.037 --> 00:09:37.720
- [Michael] It's my 29th
year cooking in the kitchen.

00:09:37.720 --> 00:09:38.770
- [Chris] I've worked with my father,

00:09:38.770 --> 00:09:40.223
close to 40 years.

00:09:41.160 --> 00:09:42.690
- [Michael] The most
important thing really,

00:09:42.690 --> 00:09:43.830
it's not about stars.

00:09:43.830 --> 00:09:44.830
It's not about that.

00:09:44.960 --> 00:09:47.240
- [Chris] I want to continue the story

00:09:47.240 --> 00:09:48.623
of my father Mario.

00:09:48.650 --> 00:09:49.810
- [Michael] It's about teaching now,

00:09:49.810 --> 00:09:51.110
showing and making connection

00:09:51.110 --> 00:09:52.753
with young people in the kitchen.

00:09:56.080 --> 00:09:56.980
- [Chris] As a merchant,

00:09:56.980 --> 00:09:58.520
he was always there for others,

00:09:58.520 --> 00:10:00.073
and he was always giving.

00:10:01.100 --> 00:10:02.283
May he rest in peace.

00:10:04.884 --> 00:10:08.634
